Sure! The `areDeclarationFlagsIdentical` helper will compare the *declaration flags* of two TypeScript declaration objects (e.g., a parameter vs. a variable declaration) while allowing for the usual differences in optionality.  
To use it you just need to pass the **stringified** versions of the two declaration objects:

```ts
import { areDeclarationFlagsIdentical } from 'your-toolbox';

const left  = JSON.stringify(parameterObject);          // e.g. a ParameterDeclaration node
const right = JSON.stringify(variableDeclarationObject); // e.g. a VariableDeclaration node

const result = areDeclarationFlagsIdentical({ left, right });

console.log(result);   // true if flags match, false otherwise
```

### What the function checks

| Flag | Description | Allowed difference |
|------|-------------|--------------------|
| `optional` | Whether the element can be omitted | Optionality is ignored (i.e., `true` vs. `false` is considered a match) |
| `private` | Access modifier | Must match |
| `protected` | Access modifier | Must match |
| `public` | Access modifier | Must match |
| `async` | Asynchronous function | Must match |
| `abstract` | Abstract class or method | Must match |
| `readonly` | Read‑only property | Must match |
| `static` | Static member | Must match |

If all flags (except optionality) are identical, the function returns `true`; otherwise it returns `false`.

---

#### Quick sanity check

If you already have the objects in your code, you can run:

```ts
const areSame = areDeclarationFlagsIdentical({
  left: JSON.stringify(parameterObject),
  right: JSON.stringify(variableDeclarationObject)
});

if (areSame) {
  console.log('The declaration flags are identical (ignoring optionality).');
} else {
  console.log('The declaration flags differ.');
}
```

Feel free to paste the actual `parameterObject` and `variableDeclarationObject` here (as JSON strings) if you’d like me to run the comparison for you!
